 {id:'pullup',unit:4,title:'What does the button say?',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'MEET',intro:'A button has four legs, but inside it is just a simple switch. The two left legs are always connected to each other, and so are the two right legs. Pressing it connects left to right. In this unit the button sits between GPIO 22 and GND.',code:'void setup() {\n  pinMode(22, INPUT_PULLUP);\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(digitalRead(22));\n  delay(50);\n}',question:'What does the Serial Monitor show while the button is not pressed?',choices:['1 (HIGH): the internal pull-up pulls the pin to 3.3 V','0 (LOW): the button is connected to GND','Random 0s and 1s: the pin is floating'],answer:0,explanation:'INPUT_PULLUP switches on a resistor of about 45 kΩ inside the ESP32 and pulls the pin HIGH. When pressed, the button connects the pin straight to GND. The button beats the weak resistor and the reading becomes LOW, or 0.',hint:'A pull-up pulls the pin up, to 3.3 V.'},
 {id:'floating',unit:4,title:'The floating pin',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'UNDERSTAND',intro:'A pin connected to nothing is “floating”. It picks up noise from the air, even from your hand nearby.',code:'void setup() {\n  pinMode(22, INPUT);   // no pull-up\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(digitalRead(22));\n  delay(50);\n}',question:'Same circuit: what do you see while the button is not pressed?',choices:['Random jumping 0s and 1s','Always 1','Always 0'],answer:0,explanation:'In INPUT mode the internal resistor is off. A released button connects the pin to nothing, so the reading changes at random. INPUT_PULLUP calms the pin down. The other option is a 10 kΩ pull-down resistor from the pin to GND with the button wired to 3.3 V; then a press reads HIGH.',hint:'What is connected to the pin while the button is not pressed?'},
 {id:'button',unit:4,title:'A button lights an LED',minutes:6,xp:40,type:'lab',mode:'button',tag:'BUILD',intro:'Your LED circuit is ready. Place the button across the gap in the middle of the breadboard. Connect GPIO 22 to one side of the button and the other side to GND. Then run the circuit, press the button and let go.',hint:'The button sits in rows e and f. Example: put it in column 10 (10–12), then connect GPIO 22 → h10 and h12 → GND. If the wire and GND end up on the same side of the button, the pin always reads LOW.'},
 {id:'bounce',unit:4,title:'One press, one count',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'bounce',tag:'MEASURE',intro:'When metal contacts touch, they bounce for a few milliseconds. The ESP32 reads the pin so fast that one press can look like several. After each change, ignore new changes for a short time: this is called debouncing.',hint:'The bounces here last less than 20 ms. The video uses 50 ms; leaving some margin is good.'},
 {id:'millis',unit:4,title:'Check the clock, don’t wait',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'CALCULATE',intro:'millis() returns the milliseconds since the board started, and it counts by itself. Instead of waiting, we check the clock every time round the loop: have 500 ms passed since the last change?',code:'unsigned long now = millis();\nif (now - lastBlink >= 500) {\n  lastBlink = now;\n  ledOn = !ledOn;\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledOn ? HIGH : LOW);\n}',question:'lastBlink is 700 and now is 1000. What happens in this loop?',choices:['Nothing: 1000 − 700 = 300, not 500 yet','The LED changes: 1000 is greater than 500','The program waits 200 ms'],answer:0,explanation:'We always subtract: now − lastBlink. 300 < 500, so the condition is false and loop() moves on without waiting. When now reaches 1200, 500 ms have passed: the LED changes and lastBlink becomes 1200. Subtraction even works when millis() rolls over to 0 after about 49.7 days.',hint:'Work out the difference in the condition: now − lastBlink.'},
 {id:'nowait',unit:4,title:'Two things at once',minutes:6,xp:60,type:'nowait',tag:'CHALLENGE',intro:'We want to listen to the button while the LED blinks. Each press stops the blinking or starts it again. First press the button briefly a few times in the delay() version. Then switch to the millis() version and catch three presses in a row without missing one.',hint:'While delay() waits, the ESP32 doesn’t look at the button at all; it is only read between the waits. In the millis() version, loop() never waits.'},
 {id:'check4',unit:4,title:'Unit 4 checkpoint',minutes:6,xp:50,type:'checkpoint',tag:'CHECKPOINT',pass:4,intro:'Comfortable with INPUT_PULLUP, debouncing and millis()? Pass this checkpoint and unit 5 opens right away.',hint:'There are no hints per question. After you check, every question shows an explanation.',questions:[
  {q:'With INPUT_PULLUP and a button to GND, which condition means “pressed”?',choices:['digitalRead(pin) == LOW','digitalRead(pin) == HIGH','analogRead(pin) > 0'],answer:0,explain:'The pull-up holds the pin HIGH; pressing connects it to GND, so it reads LOW.'},
  {q:'lastChange = 4980 and now = 5020. With a 50 ms debounce, how many more ms until a new change is accepted?',input:true,unit:'ms',answer:10,explain:'5020 − 4980 = 40 ms have passed. 50 − 40 = 10 ms to go.'},
  {q:'Why does the button feel unresponsive here?',code:'void loop() {\n  if (digitalRead(22) == LOW) blinking = !blinking;\n  digitalWrite(23, HIGH);\n  delay(1000);\n  digitalWrite(23, LOW);\n  delay(1000);\n}',choices:['The button is read only once every 2 seconds','INPUT_PULLUP is too weak','digitalWrite() blocks the button'],answer:0,explain:'The two delays freeze loop() for 2 seconds; the button is checked only once per pass.'},
  {q:'With millis(), a green LED changes state every 150 ms. How many changes in 3 seconds?',input:true,answer:20,explain:'3000 / 150 = 20 changes, no matter what else the loop does.'},
  {q:'Why write now - last >= interval instead of now >= last + interval?',choices:['It keeps working when millis() rolls over to 0','It is faster','It uses less memory'],answer:0,explain:'With unsigned subtraction the difference stays correct even after the roll-over (about 49.7 days); the addition version breaks.'}
 ]},
 {id:'pwm',unit:5,title:'Half on, half off',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'MEET',intro:'A GPIO is either fully on (3.3 V) or off (0 V). PWM switches it on and off very fast; the share of time it is on is called the duty cycle. analogWrite(pin, value) takes values from 0 to 255.',code:'void setup() {\n  pinMode(23, OUTPUT);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  analogWrite(23, 64);\n}',question:'How bright does the LED look?',choices:['About a quarter: it is on 25% of the time','Off: 64 is too small','Fully on: any value above 0 turns it on'],answer:0,explanation:'64 / 255 ≈ 25%. The pin is on for a quarter of every period. Your eye averages the fast switching, so the LED looks dimmer. On the ESP32, analogWrite() uses the LEDC hardware underneath.',hint:'What fraction of 255 is 64?'},
 {id:'dimmer',unit:5,title:'Dial in the brightness',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'pwm',tag:'PROGRAM',intro:'The LED is on GPIO 23 as before. Set the analogWrite() value to reach each target brightness. Watch the waveform: the wider the high part, the brighter the LED.',hint:'value = brightness % × 255 / 100. For 25%: 0.25 × 255 ≈ 64.'},
 {id:'rgb',unit:5,title:'Three LEDs in one',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'UNDERSTAND',intro:'An RGB LED holds a red, a green and a blue LED. With a common-cathode RGB LED the long leg goes to GND and each color gets its own PWM pin and its own 220 Ω resistor.',code:'analogWrite(RED_PIN, 255);\nanalogWrite(GREEN_PIN, 255);\nanalogWrite(BLUE_PIN, 0);',question:'Which color do you see?',choices:['Yellow','Purple','White'],answer:0,explanation:'Red and green light together look yellow. Red + blue gives purple, all three give white. With a common-anode LED the logic is inverted: 0 means full brightness.',hint:'Mix light, not paint: red + green = ?'},
 {id:'mixer',unit:5,title:'Mix the colors',minutes:5,xp:40,type:'rgb',tag:'MIX',intro:'Three sliders, three analogWrite() calls. Mix each target color on the RGB LED. Close enough counts: every channel may be off by a little.',hint:'Orange is a lot of red with half green. Cyan is green and blue. Purple is mostly blue with some red.'},
 {id:'adc',unit:5,title:'Read the knob',minutes:3,xp:20,type:'quiz',tag:'MEASURE',intro:'A potentiometer is a voltage divider: turning the knob moves its middle leg between 0 V and 3.3 V. analogRead() measures that voltage. On the ESP32 the result has 12 bits: 0 to 4095. We use GPIO 34, an ADC1 pin that also works when WiFi is on.',code:'void setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(analogRead(34));\n  delay(100);\n}',question:'The knob is exactly in the middle. Roughly what does it print?',choices:['About 2048','About 512','About 128'],answer:0,explanation:'4095 / 2 ≈ 2048. Arduino Uno boards read 10 bits (0–1023), which is why older tutorials show 512. The ESP32 ADC is not perfectly linear near its ends, so small deviations are normal.',hint:'Half of 4095.'},
 {id:'knob',unit:5,title:'A dimmer knob',minutes:6,xp:60,type:'knob',tag:'CHALLENGE',intro:'Connect the knob to the LED: read the potentiometer and turn the reading into a brightness with map(). Fill in the two missing numbers, then turn the knob from one end to the other.',hint:'map(value, fromLow, fromHigh, toLow, toHigh). The ADC reads up to 4095; analogWrite() takes up to 255.'},
 {id:'check5',unit:5,title:'Unit 5 checkpoint',minutes:6,xp:50,type:'checkpoint',tag:'CHECKPOINT',pass:4,intro:'Already know PWM, RGB mixing and analogRead()? Pass this checkpoint to finish the unit.',hint:'There are no hints per question. After you check, every question shows an explanation.',questions:[
  {q:'8-bit PWM: which analogWrite() value gives a 50% duty cycle?',input:true,answer:128,tolerance:1,explain:'255 / 2 ≈ 127.5, so 127 or 128.'},
  {q:'The potentiometer reads 3072. What does map(raw, 0, 4095, 0, 255) return?',input:true,answer:191,tolerance:1,explain:'3072 × 255 / 4095 ≈ 191.3; map() uses whole numbers, so 191.'},
  {q:'Common-cathode RGB LED: red 255, green 0, blue 255. Which color?',choices:['Purple (magenta)','Yellow','Cyan'],answer:0,explain:'Red + blue light = purple/magenta.'},
  {q:'Why does a PWM-driven LED look dim instead of flickering?',choices:['The switching is faster than your eye; you see the average','PWM lowers the voltage to a steady value','The resistor smooths the light'],answer:0,explain:'The LED really switches fully on and off, hundreds or thousands of times a second. Your eye averages it.'},
  {q:'3.3 V pin, 25% duty cycle. What is the average voltage (V)?',input:true,unit:'V',answer:0.825,tolerance:0.03,explain:'0.25 × 3.3 V = 0.825 V on average.'}
 ]}
